#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166597 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d83ab99-0e27-5989-9a57-6b4a192fa4a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "e3df676d-e1cc-584e-8b14-a6191a8d6831" :question/math-academy-id "e-8553" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)$, compute $P(X = 3)$." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{(1 - p)^{x - 1}p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nso the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})^{x - 1}(\\frac{2}{3}),\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 3) &= f(3) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{3})^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166598 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "120381af-c472-5894-8b19-da2ab61d3a7e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "f795e566-5cdf-5970-bf94-5b1c838b69f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8554" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 37\\right)$, compute $P(X < 5)$." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{(1 - p)^{x - 1}p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 37\\right)\n$$\n\nso the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{7})^{x - 1}(\\frac{3}{7}),\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 5) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) + P(X = 4) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + f(4) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{7})^{0}(\\frac{3}{7}) + (\\frac{4}{7})^{1}(\\frac{3}{7}) + (\\frac{4}{7})^{2}(\\frac{3}{7}) + (\\frac{4}{7})^{3}(\\frac{3}{7}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.893\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166599 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c566525f-9b0f-586f-ba60-277400cb25dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Not Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "c23e8b4a-dad2-5c6a-95ee-2a439343ded0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8555" :question/problem "Given that $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 15\\right)$, compute $P(2 \\leq X \\leq 3)$." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{(1 - p)^{x - 1}p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 15\\right)\n$$\n\nso the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{5})^{x - 1}(\\frac{1}{5}),\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(2 \\le X \\le 3) &= P(X ∈ {2, 3}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) \\\\\n&= f(2) + f(3) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4}{5})^{1}(\\frac{1}{5}) + (\\frac{4}{5})^{2}(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{125}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166600 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "215130d1-96ea-5f88-8de0-d73758ea8344" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "c798d6ea-a258-5a73-8cd2-366e1b3e9db0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8556" :question/problem "Given $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 56\\right)$, compute $P(X > 2)$." :question/worked-solution "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p)$, then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{(1 - p)^{x - 1}p,\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3, … \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nHere,\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 56\\right)\n$$\n\nso the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{6})^{x - 1}(\\frac{5}{6}),\\begin{vmatrix}x = 1, 2, 3… \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X > 2$. However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 2) &= P(X ∈ {1, 2}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) \\\\\n&= f(1) + f(2) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{6})^{0}(\\frac{5}{6}) + (\\frac{1}{6})^{1}(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{36}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 2) &= 1 - P(X \\le 2) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{35}{36} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{36}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
